9 #uuid "9b2d4cbb-97db-5842-8cbf-0379ee6eb626" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281209 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186278938 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281209 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186279926 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281209 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186280482 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281209 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186280511 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281209 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186280794 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281209 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281209 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281209 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186280794 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281209 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "d4bf3c23-4651-531a-8ea1-05778a87081e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281634 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281109 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281634 #edb/ref 1069 "q-163206" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281634 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281634 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the function $f(x,y)$ and the region $D$ given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=2x+2y,\\,D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 2}.\n$$\n\nAccording to the mean value theorem for double integrals, there exists a point $(a,b)\\in D$ such that the volume of the solid enclosed between the surface $z=f(x,y)$ and the $xy$-plane over the region $D$ equals the volume of the right cylinder with base $D$ and height $f(a,b).$ Find $a$ given that $b=2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281634 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f6b96aef-d640-5c31-9a3e-e83b8daeccf9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281634 #edb/ref 1074 "According to the mean value theorem for double integrals, there exists a point $(a,b)\\in D$ such that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A=f(a,b)\\cdot \\text{Area}(D).\n$$\n\nEvaluating the integral on the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{D}(2x+2y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(2x+2y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[2xy+{y}^{2}]_{y=0}^{y=2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(4x+4)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =[2{x}^{2}+4x]_{x=0}^{x=2} \\\\  & =(2\\cdot {2}^{2}+4\\cdot 2)-0 \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)=2\\cdot 2=4.\n$$\n\nAlso, we are told that $b=2.$ So, our initial equation simplifies to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}16 & =f(a,2)\\cdot 4 \\\\ 16 & =(2a+2\\cdot 2)\\cdot 4 \\\\ 4 & =2a+4 \\\\ 0 & =2a \\\\ a & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $(0,2)\\in D.$ Therefore, the answer is $a=0.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281635 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281080 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281635 #edb/ref 1069 "q-163207" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281635 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281635 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the function $f(x,y)$ and the region $D$ given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=x+y,\\,D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 3,\\,0\\le y\\le 3}.\n$$\n\nAccording to the mean value theorem for double integrals, there exists a point $(a,b)\\in D$ such that the volume of the solid enclosed between the surface $z=f(x,y)$ and the $xy$-plane over the region $D$ equals the volume of the right cylinder with base $D$ and height $f(a,b).$ Find $b$ given that $a=2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281635 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "33038417-f976-5b04-ba1c-849f23418261" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281635 #edb/ref 1074 "According to the mean value theorem for double integrals, there exists a point $(a,b)\\in D$ such that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A=f(a,b)\\cdot \\text{Area}(D).\n$$\n\nEvaluating the integral on the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{D}(x+y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{3}(x+y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}[xy+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]_{y=0}^{y=3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(3x+\\frac{9}{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =[\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{9x}{2}]_{x=0}^{x=3} \\\\  & =(\\frac{3\\cdot (3)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{9\\cdot (3)}{2})-0 \\\\  & =27.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)=3\\cdot 3=9.\n$$\n\nAlso, we are told that $a=2.$ So, our initial equation simplifies to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}27 & =f(2,b)\\cdot 9 \\\\ 27 & =(2+b)\\cdot 9 \\\\ 27 & =9b+18 \\\\ 9 & =9b \\\\ b & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $(2,1)\\in D.$ Therefore, the answer is $b=1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186428912 #edb/ref 1030 0.5 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186428912 #edb/ref 1032 "Initial Codex estimate from captured target-band crossing frequency. Selected account multivariable: 1 rises / 2 eligible passed source activities = 0.500000; nearest selected fraction = 1/2. Crossing activity numbers [1]; waits [1]. Separate account estimates: multivariable=1/2 (1/2). Selection: most observations, then lower weight, then account name; no averaging. Single-activity estimates included. Intervening target updates: True. Crossing frequency is the chosen practical estimate of encompassing coverage; a multi-band rise counts once. This does not interpret color steps as physical repetition units. Method: encompassing-report.md. Evidence: reference/mathacademy/encompassing-analysis-2026-10-11/frequency-fractions/multivariable-frequency-estimates.json, pair 3147->1112. All account evidence retained in the prepared import's selected-relationships.json." #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186428912 #edb/ref 1036 #edb/ref 17592186050101 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186428913 #edb/ref 1030 0.5 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186428913 #edb/ref 1032 "Initial Codex estimate from captured target-band crossing frequency. Selected account multivariable: 1 rises / 2 eligible passed source activities = 0.500000; nearest selected fraction = 1/2. Crossing activity numbers [1]; waits [1]. Separate account estimates: multivariable=1/2 (1/2). Selection: most observations, then lower weight, then account name; no averaging. Single-a